Like I've already written giant ants are staple in fantasy games, but so 
are relistically working weapons. I have nothing against fantasy 
monsters, nor magic working because that is how fantasy works, but I do 
not like nonmagical weapons work differently than in real life.

If you really think that real world physics are no good way to justify 
the way things work in NetHack then why are you not condemning:

fragile items breaking when dropped from height,
Newton's first law working under low friction,
heavier items doing more damage than light ones,
gravity effects, need to eat, vision system, lighting,
etc.

There are hundreds of effects in NetHack that are based on real life 
physics and mainly because people assume that things would work that way.

This whole discussion started with me telling that in certain situations 
it is either impossible or not recommended to melee a monster and that 
the canonical answer in those situations is: throw rocks.

I assumed that sling would add to the damage of rocks, based on real 
life physics, and wondered why the canonical answer isn't use sling to 
fire rocks.

Now I know why that is not the canonical answer: because slings don't 
work in NetHack like they work in real life and in any other fantasy 
game I've played. My assumption was in error. But why was it? Almost any 
similiar situation in NetHack works like one would assume and the reason 
is that the DevTeam has coded it to work that way. Newton's first law 
under low friction is not just something that happens in the game, it is 
deliberately coded to happen to mimic the real physics.

The last time I ask you: What have you against making slings usable? 
Usable slings would enrich the game not take anything away from it.

